# Minimize Read/Write Cost (the free-plan budget)

We run on Cloudflare's free plan and want to stay there as the app grows. Cost
control is not a late-stage optimization pass; it is a design input on every
feature and every edit, the same way correctness is.

## The ceilings (know them cold)

- **D1:** ~5,000,000 rows READ/day, ~100,000 rows written/day, 5GB storage.
  Limits reset daily; exceeding them makes D1 reject queries until reset.
- **Workers:** ~100,000 requests/day. This often bites BEFORE the D1 read cap.
- **D1 bills rows SCANNED, not rows returned.** An unindexed scan over a 5,000
  row table costs 5,000 rows read even if it returns a single row. Indexes cut
  rows scanned; they add write and storage cost, so index the hot paths, not
  every column.
- **D1 per-query caps:** 100 bound parameters, 50 queries per Worker invocation.

## The rule

**Every query on a hot path must be designed to scan the fewest rows possible,
and every feature must lower total daily reads, not just add to them.**

## How (automatic on every feature)

- **Schema follows the query.** Enumerate the access patterns first, then shape
  tables and indexes so the hot reads are index seeks, not scans. Reach for
  covering indexes, partial indexes (`WHERE deleted_at IS NULL`), and explicit
  range bounds (`col >= ?lo AND col < ?hi`) instead of `LIKE '%x%'`.
- **Estimate before you commit.** For each hot query, state rows-scanned per call
  and rows/day at 100 / 1k / 10k users. If it scans the table, redesign it before
  it ships: add an index, denormalize a counter, precompute, or move the work
  off the hot path.
- **One round trip.** Batch reads; annotate a result set with a single bounded
  `IN (...)` lookup rather than N+1; keep it under the 100-parameter cap. Fewer
  Worker requests matters as much as fewer D1 rows. A persistent websocket beats
  polling; route-load refresh beats a dedicated poll endpoint.
- **Improve old queries as you pass through.** A new requirement is also a chance
  to make an existing query read fewer rows. That improvement is part of the new
  feature's work, not a separate chore (see `redesign-for-the-ideal`).
- **Never trade away a feature to save reads if a redesign saves them instead.**
  The target is the same capability at lower cost, found by rethinking the shape,
  not by dropping the capability.

## Escape hatch (the red button)

If read volume ever genuinely threatens the budget, the planned move is to push
read-heavy surfaces (catalogs, search, feeds) onto a per-device SQLite manifest
the client queries offline, refreshed periodically. Reads then collapse to
manifest generation rather than per-interaction queries, and full-text search
runs client-side. Realtime rides one websocket opened at login for all message
kinds, not a socket per feature. Full plan in `docs/realtime-and-scaling.md`.
Reach for this when measured reads approach the ceiling, not preemptively.
